\title[The uniform threshold coupling on a finite site set]
{The Uniform Threshold Coupling on a Finite Site Set:\\
Bernoulli Realisation, Strict Monotonicity,\\
Russo's Formula and the Harris Inequality}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{5 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $\iota$ be a finite set of \emph{sites} and let $x=(x_v)_{v\in\iota}$ be
independent random variables, each uniform on $[0,1]$.  For a threshold
$p\in[0,1]$ define the configuration $\Theta_p(x)\in\{0,1\}^\iota$ by declaring
$v$ \emph{open} precisely when $x_v\le p$.  We develop in complete detail, with
no asymptotic or infinite-volume input, the theory of this \emph{uniform
threshold coupling}.  We prove: (i) the law of $\Theta_p(x)$ is exactly the
Bernoulli product measure of density $p$, with fibre formula
$\Prob(\Theta_p(x)=\eta)=p^{|\Op(\eta)|}(1-p)^{|\Cl(\eta)|}$, so all densities
are realised simultaneously on one probability space; (ii) the coupling is
pointwise monotone in $p$, whence increasing events have nested key sets and
nondecreasing Bernoulli polynomials; (iii) for an increasing event that is
nonempty and omits the all-closed configuration the Bernoulli polynomial is
\emph{strictly} increasing on $(0,1)$, via an explicit positive-volume box in
key space; (iv) the finite Russo formula, expressing the derivative of the
Bernoulli polynomial as the sum of pivotal probabilities, with a sharp
criterion for strict positivity; (v) the Harris/FKG inequality, based on the
exact log-modularity of the Bernoulli weight, together with the
increasing--decreasing negative correlation form, the finite-family versions on
both sides, and the product form of the square-root trick; and
(vi) applications to horizontal crossings of the $n\times n$ square grid and a
complete bond analogue on the edge set of an arbitrary finite graph.  Exact
rational values of the crossing probability, its derivative, and the
half-probability density are reported for $n\le4$, together with falsifiable
conjectures on their asymptotics.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{percolation, threshold coupling, Bernoulli measure, increasing event,
